{
  "_comment": "Normalized readings of the sibling fixtures, asserted by BOTH the Go suite (cmd/shuttle_remotes_test.go) and the Elixir suite (daemon/test/shuttle/remotes_test.exs). Two readers, one expectation file: a default that drifts in one language fails in both. `port: 0` means absent (Go's zero value; Elixir's nil). `ssh: \"\"` means the entry has NO ssh path at all — ssh defaults to the name only for port-forwarded entries, so a bare `url` remote the operator gave no `ssh` is one the recovery cascade must not try to revive over ssh. `https_proxy` is the document-level `defaults.https_proxy` after parsing, as a {host, port} pair rather than a rendered string: \"localhost:1055\" and \"localhost:01055\" are the same endpoint but different strings, so a string expectation could not say which normalization it meant. {\"host\": \"\", \"port\": 0} means no proxy configured. Host carries no IPv6 brackets. The rejected-input table lives in each suite as a unit test, mirrored line for line, since a fixture file can only hold readings that succeed.",
  "canonical.json": {
    "launchd_label_prefix": "com.example",
    "remotes": [
      {
        "name": "hub-a",
        "url": "http://127.0.0.1:4001",
        "ssh": "hub-a-login",
        "display": "hub-a (site A)",
        "port": 4001,
        "remote_port": 4200,
        "poll_interval_ms": 5000,
        "request_timeout_ms": 2000,
        "stale_multiplier": 4,
        "label": "com.example.shuttle-tunnel-hub-a"
      },
      {
        "name": "hub-b",
        "url": "http://127.0.0.1:4004",
        "ssh": "hub-b",
        "display": "hub-b",
        "port": 4004,
        "remote_port": 4000,
        "poll_interval_ms": 9000,
        "request_timeout_ms": 30000,
        "stale_multiplier": 6,
        "label": "com.example.shuttle-tunnel-hub-b"
      }
    ],
    "https_proxy": {
      "host": "",
      "port": 0
    }
  },
